# Git Workflow (Optimal Robotics Lab)

Goal: keep `main` stable, make every change traceable, and let future students pick up the project. Avoid bureaucracy.

## Branches
- Never commit directly to `main`. It's protected, so every change reaches it through a Pull Request.
- Use one branch per feature, fix, experiment, or major change, named `<type>/<short-kebab-description>`:
  - `feature/motor-model`, `fix/can-timeout`, `refactor/config-loading`.
- Keep each branch to one logical change and merge it quickly. Long-lived branches drift.
- Don't use forks. Lab members push branches to the shared repo.

## Starting work
```bash
git switch main
git pull
git switch -c feature/my-change
```
On longer branches, merge in the latest `main` periodically.

## Commits
- Write messages in the imperative mood and describe one logical change: `Add motor torque-speed model`.
- Stage specific files rather than everything at once when unrelated or generated files are present, such as exported `*.xml` models or `.venv`.

## Pull Requests
```bash
git push -u origin feature/my-change
```
- Open a PR targeting `main`, with a short description of **what changed and why**. The PR is the record future maintainers will read.
- Review is encouraged for large, architectural, or shared-impact changes, but it isn't required. Authors may merge their own PRs.
- Delete the branch after merging.

## Agent behavior
- Never commit, push, merge, or delete a branch unless the user asks.
- Before starting work on `main` or an unrelated branch, suggest a suitably named branch.
- Never force-push to `main`.
